Phase 3A 10/25/2026

1) 
Decision: Store all money values as decimal
Where: Models/Product.cs (Price), Models/Order.cs (SubTotal, Discount, Fees, Tax, Total), Models/OrderItem.cs (UnitPrice, LineTotal), Models/AppSettings.cs (tax rate, rush fee, plan-ahead discount).
What: Every price, fee, discount, and total is held in a decimal property so the calculations regarding dollars and cents are accurate rather than drifting with binary floating-point rounding.
Why: Olive Branch Bakes charges real money (pies at $22.00, plan-ahead discount of $0.50, rush fee of $5.00, tax at 8.5%). My Variant Card (P5) needs exact half-dollar discounts and fee math. decimal matches how currency works and matches the Phase 3 rule that money must not use double or float.
Alt. Rejected: Using double for prices, not clean for binary floating point.
Source/test: I wrote this myself as part of completing the model stubs. Proved by hand-calculated rows T1 and T3 (subtotal $50.50, tax and totals to the cent).

Phase 3B 10/29/2026

1) Decision: Filter the menu in ProductService so seasonal items only appear inside their season dates
Where: Services/ProductService.cs (GetAvailableProducts), Models/Product.cs (IsInSeason)
What: Available products are those marked available and in season for the selected menu date. Gingerbread loaf uses Nov 15–Jan 5 (month/day) so it shows or hides when the customer changes “Menu as of.”
Why: Variant Card digit 4 requires Gingerbread loaf only in season, with dates stored on the product (menu data), not hard-coded in a form.
Alt. rejected: Hiding the item only in the form with an if on the date picker. That puts a business rule in the UI and is harder to test or reuse.
Source/test: Written for Checkpoint 3B. Proved by setting menu date to 12/01 (Gingerbread appears) and 06/01 (Gingerbread disappears).

Phase 3C/3D 11/9/2026

1) Decision: P5 pricing rule implemented into PricingService/O1 Pie capcity rule implemented into SchedulingService
Where: Services/PricingService.cs (CalculateDiscount, CalculateFees, GetBreakdown), Services/SchedulingService.cs (CanAcceptOrder, CountPiesForDate), called from Services/OrderService.cs and Forms/OrderSummaryForm.cs
What: PricingService applies $0.50 off each pie/cake when pickup is 7+ days out, a $5.00 rush fee under 48 hours, and 8.5% tax on the discounted subtotal plus fees. SchedulingService blocks a pickup date when pies already booked plus the cart would go over 12, and returns how many pie slots remain.
Why: Variant Card digits 0 and 8 require O1 and P5. Keeping rules in services matches the Phase 3 rule that business logic must not live only in UI events, and the same methods can be reused for summary, place order, and tests T1–T7.
Alt rejected: Calculating discount, tax, and pie limits inside btnPlaceOrder_Click. Faster to type once, but hard to test against the hand table and easy to duplicate wrong on another form.
Source/test: Proved by T1 (3-day pickup, total $54.79, no discount), T3 (7-day pickup, $0.50 off, total $54.25), and T7 (order blocked when pies would exceed 12).

